---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import FoundationsLayout from "../../components/FoundationsLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import componentModule from "../../../../src/watershed/component.gleam?raw";
import componentTests from "../../../../test/watershed/component_test.gleam?raw";

const descriptorType = excerpt(
  componentModule,
  "pub opaque type Descriptor(context, running) {",
);
const notesDefinition = excerpt(
  componentTests,
  "fn notes_decoder() -> decode.Decoder(NotesConfig) {",
  "fn notes_descriptor() -> component.Descriptor(String, String) {",
);
const catalogType = excerpt(
  componentModule,
  "pub opaque type Catalog(context, running) {",
);
const catalogExample = excerpt(
  componentTests,
  "pub fn catalog_holds_different_config_types_test() -> Nil {",
);
const registerFn = excerpt(componentModule, "pub fn register(");
const findFn = excerpt(componentModule, "pub fn find(");
const validateFn = excerpt(componentModule, "pub fn validate_config(");
const startFn = excerpt(componentModule, "pub fn start(");
const portsFn = excerpt(componentModule, "pub fn ports(");
---

<FoundationsLayout
  slug="components"
  description="Watershed describes reusable component kinds with versioned descriptors that hide config types behind decoders and start closures, while one catalog gives the host a common context, running value, port list, and precise lookup errors."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>A descriptor is the catalog boundary</h2>
    <p>
      A host wants one catalog of component kinds. Gleam requires one type for
      all values in that catalog. Configuration breaks that uniformity: notes
      might need a placeholder string, while a counter needs an integer step.
      A broad config union would expose each component's private choices to the
      host.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>Descriptor(context, running)</code> keeps the config type out of
      its public type. The descriptor holds the decoder and start closure that
      know that type. The catalog sees the two types all components in one host
      must share: what the host passes in, and what it gets back.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={descriptorType} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Each component keeps its config type</h2>
    <p>
      You still write ordinary typed Gleam inside a component. This notes
      definition decodes JSON into <code>NotesConfig</code>, then starts with
      that exact type. <code>component.descriptor</code> closes over both
      functions and removes the config type from the host-facing API.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={notesDefinition} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        source: test/watershed/component_test.gleam
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The two <code>String</code> parameters in the return type are the host
      contract in this test: a string context goes in and a string running
      value comes back. Another descriptor in the same catalog can hide a
      different config type, but it must honor that same host contract.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>One catalog, exact versions</h2>
    <p>
      A catalog keys each descriptor by its stable kind and integer format
      version. Several versions of one kind can coexist. Registering the same
      pair twice returns <code>DuplicateRegistration</code>. Lookup requires
      the requested version.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={catalogType} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={registerFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={catalogExample} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        notes and counter configs differ; the host contract matches
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      With exact lookup, a host gets two useful failure states.{" "}
      <code>NotRegistered(kind)</code> means it knows no version of the kind.
      <code>UnsupportedVersion(kind, requested, available)</code> means it can
      name the versions it does know. A host can report the missing kind or the
      unsupported version without guessing.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={findFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The catalog requires an exact version.</strong> Register an
        older descriptor when a host still supports it. The host owns
        migration and any fallback or code-loading policy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Validation and startup stay separate</h2>
    <p>
      <code>validate_config</code> runs the descriptor's decoder and stops
      there. A builder or manifest reader can check stored JSON without
      opening subscriptions or creating a running component.{" "}
      <code>start</code> decodes the same JSON, then calls the typed start
      closure.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={validateFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={startFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The error tells you which side failed. <code>InvalidConfig</code> reports
      the kind, version, and JSON decode error. <code>StartFailed</code> reports
      the same identity plus the reason returned by component code. The host
      can report the decode or startup failure.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The port list is the component's public wiring surface</h2>
    <p>
      A descriptor also includes erased port metadata.{" "}
      <code>component.ports</code> exposes enough information for a host to
      list ports and validate stored connections without knowing each payload
      type. Component code keeps the typed input and output values that
      perform encoding, decoding, and direct connection checks.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={portsFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Types protect code written together. Descriptors let the host validate
      data read later from a catalog or graph.{" "}
      <a href="/foundations/ports">The next sheet</a> follows both forms through
      a connection and a dispatch plan.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>You supply the component runtime</h3>
    <p>
      Use this module to define descriptors, build catalogs, validate config,
      look up versions, and call a start closure. Your host must persist its
      instance manifest, assign child maps, mount views, maintain
      subscriptions, stop instances, and migrate data. Watershed ships the
      catalog foundation. You supply that host runtime.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">The contract</span>
    <p>
      A descriptor hides one component's config type behind its decoder and
      start closure. A catalog keeps those unlike definitions together under
      one host contract, looks them up by exact kind and version, and exposes
      their erased port metadata. Next: <a href="/foundations/ports">how those
      ports become a checked graph and an origin-aware delivery plan</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</FoundationsLayout>
